WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.690
Film Courage: You talked about switching up
cognitive processes.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:07.780
Are there certain movies that seem to do that
in a 2-hour span where you are gong from one

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:08.830
emotion to the next?

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.980
Dr. Connie Shears, Author and Professor - Crean
at Chapman University: Sure, there are lots

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.129
of movies that fast forward your emotions.

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:25.720
Most of your JACK REACHER kind of solve-the-crime,
stay-out-of-danger, moving at a very quick

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.320
pace.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:34.940
Those movies tend not to involve much humor
or much regret.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:40.800
Those movies tend to be based on fear and
anticipation so they keep kind of a higher

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.730
heart rate going while you are watching.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:54.140
The movie that are more psychological or involve
an aspect of reflection, thinking about your

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.140
memories.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:06.409
So 500 DAYS or 50 FIRST DATES, those kind
of movies that portray people who have lost

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:13.479
memory or are not forming memories properly
are very engaging, real successful in the

00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:15.270
box office.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:23.450
More engaging for an audience who may not
be a hero or a daredevil or that kind of a

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:32.079
person, maybe can watch those but has a lingering
appreciate for someone whose mind is not working

00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:34.880
exactly the way their’s is.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:45.480
So lots of movies come at different cognitive
processes and in forming a story around not

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:52.600
working cognitive processes, some kind of
brain injury that has changed that person,

00:01:52.600 --> 00:02:00.659
you get a lot of fascination from the viewer’s
perspective because everybody relates to forgetting

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:03.320
at some time or another.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.409
Everybody has had a forgetful experience.

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:15.430
What is it like to not be able to form a memory
so that you live in a forgotten state?

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:20.550
Those are some interesting psychological brain
processes that have been presented and done

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.770
well in the theaters.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.440
One of the aspects that we point out in [Connie
and Paul Joseph Gulino’s book] The Science

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:36.120
of Screenwriting is how skilled screenwriters
build in the contrast of emotions.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:47.030
So there’s been some studies done and researchers
have used eye trackers in audiences such that

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:55.050
they can tell where the audiences eyes are
focused on the screen during which kinds of

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.050
movies.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:04.610
And because we can measure like how long the
eyes stay on the character and how often the

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:13.650
eyes move off of the character we can translate
those eye movements into attentional factors.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:19.760
And we now understand that timing is critical.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:30.870
So a viewer can take about 3 minutes of tension
of that kind of bond (fighting with the shark

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:38.810
or whatever that is) and there needs to be
a break or the eyes are going to soccolld.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.130
You can take too much tension so we need a
relief.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.680
So if you’ll notice in the [JAMES] BOND
movies it’s really brief.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:54.480
It’s about a minute and a half where it’s
bad stuff kind of tension and then there’s

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:04.400
a segue whether that means it’s a further
away shot, long distance shot or a scan to

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:13.890
the boat that is coming to the rescue or whatever
interruption in order to give the nerves,

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:20.190
the brain processes that are building tension
in that watcher a break.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:30.070
And it has to be at least at 30-60-90 second
breaks to scan away before you can reengage

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:33.900
in a tight emotional situation.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:42.560
So we know those processes are happening in
daily life and that when people go to a movie

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:54.810
they anticipate experiencing deeper tension,
more dramatic exchanges in order to maintain

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.060
their attention.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:03.180
You still have to give those interactive breaks
so that there is a release and then a reengagement

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.850
in the tension and conflict.

